[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> Okay, so I have this problem where I need to find the smallest possible value of the norm of the sum of two vectors a and b. The norms of a and b are given as 3 and 14, respectively. Hmm, vectors and their magnitudes. I remember that when dealing with vectors, there's something about the triangle inequality. Let me think about this.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> The triangle inequality states that the magnitude of the sum of two vectors is less than or equal to the sum of their magnitudes. So, the maximum value of ||a + b|| is ||a|| + ||b||, which in this case would be 3 + 14 = 17. But the problem is asking for the smallest possible value. So, I need to recall how the angle between the vectors affects this.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> Oh right! The dot product formula. The square of the norm of the sum of two vectors can be expanded using the dot product. So, ||a + b||² = ||a||² + ||b||² + 2(a · b). The term (a · b) is the dot product, which equals ||a|| ||b|| cos(theta), where theta is the angle between vectors a and b.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> So, to minimize ||a + b||, I need to minimize ||a + b||². Since the square of a real number is always non-negative, the smallest value occurs when the dot product is as negative as possible because that would subtract the maximum possible from the sum of the squares.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> Wait, because the dot product can be negative, if I make it negative, the term 2(a·b) would be negative, thus making the entire expression ||a||² + ||b||² + 2(a·b) as small as possible. So, to minimize ||a + b||, I need to maximize the negative value of the dot product.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> Wait, no, wait. Let me think again. Let me write down the equation:
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> ||a + b||² = ||a||² + ||b||² + 2(a · b)
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> = 3² + 14² + 2*(3*14*cos(theta))
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> = 9 + 196 + 84 cos(theta)
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> = 205 + 84 cos(theta)
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> So, to minimize ||a + b||, which equals sqrt(205 + 84 cos(theta)), I need to minimize this expression. The square root function is increasing, so the minimal value occurs when the expression inside is as small as possible.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> What's the range of cos(theta)? It can be between -1 and 1. So, to make the term 84 cos(theta) as small as possible, I need cos(theta) as small as possible, which is -1. Because 84*(-1) = -84. So, the minimal value of ||a + b||² would be 205 + (-84) = 121.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> Therefore, the minimal ||a + b|| is sqrt(121) = 11.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> Wait, so that's the answer. Let me just recap. The minimal value occurs when the angle between a and b is 180 degrees, meaning they are pointing in opposite directions. So, the vectors subtract each other, leading to the minimal possible sum. That makes sense because adding two vectors in opposite directions gives the smallest possible magnitude.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:26:19,760 >> Another way to think about it is using the formula for the magnitude of the difference of two vectors, which would be the maximum when they point in the same direction. So, yeah, the minimal sum is when they are antiparallel.
