[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> Okay, so I need to find the remainder when the polynomial \(2x^4 + 11x^3 - 42x^2 - 60x + 47\) is divided by \(x^2 + 7x - 5\). Hmm, I remember from algebra that when you divide one polynomial by another, you can express it as \(dividend = divisor \times quotient + remainder\). The remainder should have a lower degree than the divisor. Since the divisor here is degree 2, the remainder should be a linear polynomial, which means it should look like \(ax + b\), where \(a\) and \(b\) are constants I need to find.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> I think the best way to approach this is by using polynomial long division or synthetic division. But since the divisor is a quadratic, synthetic division might be a bit complicated because it's usually for linear divisors. So maybe polynomial long division is better here.
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> Let me set up the division: divide \(2x^4 + 11x^3 - 42x^2 - 60x + 47\) by \(x^2 + 7x - 5\). 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> First step is to see how many times the leading term of the divisor, which is \(x^2\), goes into the leading term of the dividend, which is \(2x^4\). So, \(2x^4 / x^2 = 2x^2\). That means the first term in the quotient will be \(2x^2\). 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> Now, I multiply the entire divisor by \(2x^2\): \(2x^2 \times (x^2 + 7x -5) = 2x^4 + 14x^3 -10x^2\). 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> Subtracting this from the original dividend:
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> \( (2x^4 + 11x^3 -42x^2 -60x +47) - (2x^4 +14x^3 -10x^2) \)
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> Let's compute this term by term:
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> - \(2x^4 - 2x^4 = 0\)
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> - \(11x^3 -14x^3 = -3x^3\)
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> - \(-42x^2 - (-10x^2) = -32x^2\)
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> So now the new dividend is \(-3x^3 -32x^2 -60x +47\).
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> 
[INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:3287] 2026-10-26 20:28:21,037 >> Next, I take the leading term of this new dividend, which is \(-3x^3\), and divide it by the leading term of the divisor, \(x^2\), which gives \(-3x\). So the next term in the quotient is \(-3x\).
